{
  "case_id": "2008-08-479",
  "document_hash": "99248ed6d0b508eb63d611f4c1d39ef912bddf721d3d84e16bcb1dbc578f35a0",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2008-08-479",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99248ed6d0b508eb63d611f4c1d39ef912bddf721d3d84e16bcb1dbc578f35a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99248ed6d0b5-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The search of Savana Redding in this case was constitutional because Mr. Wilson had reason to suspect that she possessed contraband which posed a health and safety risk. Therefore, searching any place where she might be reasonably hiding that contraband was constitutionally permissible."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2008-08-479",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99248ed6d0b508eb63d611f4c1d39ef912bddf721d3d84e16bcb1dbc578f35a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99248ed6d0b5-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We will hear argument first this morning in Case 08-479, Safford Unifed School District v. Redding."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-08-479",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99248ed6d0b508eb63d611f4c1d39ef912bddf721d3d84e16bcb1dbc578f35a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99248ed6d0b5-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But I -- I think you're really caught in -- in a dilemma here. Your answer suggests that you would not have allowed a cavity search in this case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2008-08-479",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99248ed6d0b508eb63d611f4c1d39ef912bddf721d3d84e16bcb1dbc578f35a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99248ed6d0b5-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "another bright-line rule that I think you're assuming. You -- you said in the course of describing the justification for this search that one -- one of the points of justification was that there was a health and safety risk. And I assume from the way you put it that you are grouping every drug, prescription or over the counter, as posing a health and safety risk; is that correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2008-08-479",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "99248ed6d0b508eb63d611f4c1d39ef912bddf721d3d84e16bcb1dbc578f35a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99248ed6d0b5-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: When was the -- when was she detained there in relation to the lunchtime period, which was when the other student had said that all the kids were going to take these pills?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Plaintiff",
      "case_id": "2008-08-479",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "99248ed6d0b508eb63d611f4c1d39ef912bddf721d3d84e16bcb1dbc578f35a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99248ed6d0b5-0001-0003",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the plaintiff argued the very thing, that she was detained for hours."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2008-08-479",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "99248ed6d0b508eb63d611f4c1d39ef912bddf721d3d84e16bcb1dbc578f35a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99248ed6d0b5-0001-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF DAVID O'NEIL ON BEHALF OF THE UNITED STATES, AS AMICUS CURIAE, SUPPORTING REVERSAL MR. O'NEIL: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: Intrusive body searches in the school context implicate fundamentally different expectations of privacy than other -- other kinds of searches. For that reason, they require greater justification under the Fourth Amendment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2008-08-479",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "99248ed6d0b508eb63d611f4c1d39ef912bddf721d3d84e16bcb1dbc578f35a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99248ed6d0b5-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Does that make a difference in the permissibility of the search and the reasonableness of the search and the scope of the search?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Wolf",
      "case_id": "2008-08-479",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "99248ed6d0b508eb63d611f4c1d39ef912bddf721d3d84e16bcb1dbc578f35a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99248ed6d0b5-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "No, it does not. It does not in this case, because without a suspicion that you're going to find the object --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. WOLF",
      "case_id": "2008-08-479",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99248ed6d0b508eb63d611f4c1d39ef912bddf721d3d84e16bcb1dbc578f35a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99248ed6d0b5-0003-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But the \"not beyond human experience\" standard is not the standard that governs whether the Fourth Amendment is violated."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE ALITO",
      "case_id": "2008-08-479",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99248ed6d0b508eb63d611f4c1d39ef912bddf721d3d84e16bcb1dbc578f35a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99248ed6d0b5-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is that a question of law or is that a question that goes to the trier of fact, whether you could infer reasonable suspicion about the presence of the pills in those locations?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "JUSTICE SCALIA",
      "case_id": "2008-08-479",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99248ed6d0b508eb63d611f4c1d39ef912bddf721d3d84e16bcb1dbc578f35a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99248ed6d0b5-0003-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is probable cause a jury question too?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. WRIGHT",
      "case_id": "2008-08-479",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99248ed6d0b508eb63d611f4c1d39ef912bddf721d3d84e16bcb1dbc578f35a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99248ed6d0b5-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The reality is that we've gotten past the reasonable at its inception prong, and we're now on the scope. And I think everyone concedes that. And, as to the scope, I can tell you that if they found uncut heroin or small-caliber bullets in this case, they would have to reach the same results. And that is unacceptable in the school setting. There's too much at risk here when you're talking about the custodial and tutelary responsibilities this Court has time and again recognized as the most important elements in upholding the three search cases, student search cases, that this Court has upheld."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amicus brief",
      "case_id": "2008-08-479",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99248ed6d0b508eb63d611f4c1d39ef912bddf721d3d84e16bcb1dbc578f35a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99248ed6d0b5-0003-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 63,
        "start": 63
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There is an amicus brief that cites 189 school districts which have substantially limited strip searches, which proves the Ingraham v. Wright theory, which is that it will be taken care of at the local level."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-08-479",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "99248ed6d0b508eb63d611f4c1d39ef912bddf721d3d84e16bcb1dbc578f35a0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-99248ed6d0b5-0003-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
